ertarian Colt Shelby may pull some anti-Sanders votes, but Love remains well short of a majority coalition"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Sanders: a deep-red state, an incumbent governor, unanimous Safe/Solid R ratings, and markets giving Democrats only 2-3%. The lone August poll and coverage of waning approval suggest a somewhat closer contest than the ratings imply, so the expected margin is trimmed slightly, but not nearly enough to threaten the outcome.", "watch": "Any follow-up polling confirming that the tighter August numbers were real, rather than a seasonal dip in Sanders's approval, would be the clearest signal of a genuine shift.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Sarah Huckabee Sanders (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Sarah Huckabee Sanders seeks a second term in a state that leans heavily Republican, facing Democrat Fredrick Love and Libertarian Colt Shelby. Every major rating outfit calls the race Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Sanders roughly a 97 percent chance of winning. The state's partisan lean and the power of incumbency both work strongly in her favor.\n\nStill, the race has shown signs of life. An August Hendrix College poll of likely voters put Sanders ahead by only five points, and recent coverage has highlighted softening approval for the governor and a closer-than-expected contest. Love has campaigned against the state's school voucher program. Those signals narrow the expected margin somewhat, but the structural advantages remain firmly with the incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0236, "margin": -11.99}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0374, "margin": -11.5}]}